256. **[PARKED needs-human-review iteration 362 — draft PR #515, implementation `76553889`, independent MiniMax FAIL 85; local criterion UNMET; D-WORLD-111 validation and D-WORLD-112 credential incident; below every D-WORLD-81 release row; in D-WORLD-108's scope after row 255 (attended 2026-11-06); filed attended 2026-11-06 from the home service's restarts] w-daemon-startup-under-load** · clause-3 (MET; availability) · **UNDER HEAVY HOST LOAD THE SERVICE CANNOT START: EVERY EXEC PROFILE IS LEFT OUT, THEN THE REGISTRY BOOTSTRAP TIMES OUT AND THE SERVICE STOPS.** On 2026-11-03, `ailang-worldd service start` ran on a 16-core Mac at a 1-minute load of ~110–130. The load came from Blender and about ten `ailang` release builds in another session. Startup logged `exec profile "<p>" left out: exec startup probe failed … (7 of 7 arms): … the startup budget is spent; stderr ""` for all five profiles. It then logged `daemon startup failed at registry-bootstrap: cannot bootstrap world/epoch-registry/v1 with release "AILANG v0.41.0": context deadline exceeded`, and the start ended with `did not answer /v1/health within 30 s; service stopped`. A second start at load ~100 failed the same way. A third, at load 16, succeeded with every profile probed. At episode bind, the same load also gave `workspace tools unavailable … policy summary: broker: handler subprocess timed out after 10s`. Candidates:
    - give the registry bootstrap its own deadline, not one shared with the probes;
    - retry a profile whose probe ran out of budget, as opposed to failing an arm, with backoff after startup. A budget-spent probe is not a confinement failure, and fail-closed still holds while it waits;
    - name left-out profiles in `/v1/health` and in `doctor`. Today `doctor` was all green while exec was failing;
    - make an exec call for a left-out profile refuse with that name.
  Interacts with row 250 (the same probe) and row 233 (D-WORLD-105). Design and quorum first, because this touches the fail-closed probe.
257. **[NEXT — below every D-WORLD-81 release row; in D-WORLD-108's scope after row 256 (attended 2026-11-06); filed attended 2026-11-06 from episode worldd-2611031015] w-exec-cache-seed-at-bind** · clause-5 (MET; agent UX) · **THE GOCACHE SEED IS CLONED ON THE EPISODE'S FIRST EXEC CALL, INSIDE THAT CALL'S 9-SECOND BUDGET.** `prepareCache` (`host/broker/handlers_exec.go`) clones the operator's seed: 225 MB for `ailang-world` (`cp -cR`, or `cp -a --reflink=auto`). It runs under `execSeedBudget(ctx)`, the remaining call budget. That took 0.69 s on an idle Mac. Under load it competes with the command it serves, and a failure repeats on every call. Build: clone the seed when the episode's exec handler is bound, or at `episode start`, before the agent runs. Report a seed failure there, with the row-255 reason, and keep the per-call path as a check that the copy exists. Measure the clone time at bind under package-parallel load, and say what bound it gets. Tests: the first call spawns no seed copy when bind succeeded, and a bind-time failure is visible in the episode report.
